nce shows no incidents so it could wait. The deadline is a concern if the next sitting falls too late, but it's policy-level rather than constitutional, so it's secondary to the ranking. Let me settle my position on readiness and sequencing.\n\nI'm working through why P5 has to come before P3: under the current \"all 5\" rule, the Steward's recusal would leave only four voters, so unanimity could never be reached, and redefining it as \"all excluding abstentions\" is what makes the later item workable.\n\nI'm ranking P5 first, then P3, then P2, so the Council defines its own decision rules before deciding under them. P6 can wait for the next sitting.\n\nI'm settling on P6 and P44 for a later sitting, since they're practically tied together and the composition question affects how the deliberation feels. I'm also weighing whether three substantive items is a realistic load for the first run of this mechanism, and I think it is, though ambitious. The community's critiques of the suggested ordering deserve a real answer too, so I'll note that before I commit.\n\nI'm settling the order as P5, then P3, and P2 third, with capacity at three. I'm also questioning whether P2 should outrank P3, since P2 has waited over a month.\n\nI'm now weighing whether P2 should outrank P3, since P2 has been eligible for over a month. I still think P3 comes first: it addresses a live conflict of interest and was deferred from GOV-2026-0005 with a near-term commitment. The appeals system works with workarounds, so P2's dishonesty around the fee and second appeal matters, but it isn't broken.\n\nI'm weighing whether to raise the P6 question with the Steward, since he wants it heard this sitting and my ranking leaves it out. The argument I'd make is that changing the Council's composition in the middle of deciding whether to limit its own power, and how to change its decision rules, should happen before or after that sequence rather than during it.\n\nI'm settling on a position: proceed with 